## Pip Tools
pip is the standard package management system for Python used to install, update, and manage additional software packages that are not part of the standard Python library. The name is a recursive acronym standing for "Pip Installs Packages". It downloads and installs tools, frameworks, and libraries directly from the Python Package Index (PyPI)—the official online repository for public open-source Python packages.

### How pip works 
Instead of manually visiting websites, downloading files, and dealing with technical installations, you use a command-line interface. pip automatically grabs the code and resolves dependencies, meaning it will also download any other packages your chosen package needs to run properly.

### Check Installation of pip
```bash
    python -m pip --version
```

### Install a package
```bash
    python -m pip install pakage_name
    python -m pip install package_name==1.2.3
```

### Upgrade a package
```bash
    python -m pip install --upgrade package_name
```

### List package 
```bash
python -m pip list
```

### Uninstall package
```bash
    python -m pip uninstall package_name
```

### Install all dependencies/requirements for a project
```bash
    python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```
- its a good practice to hardpine the version number
- To see all package install we use pip freeze
```bash
    python -m pip freeze
```

### Pip Tools
Pip tools is a popular set of command-line utilities for managing Python project dependencies and ensuring reproducible builds. It is designed to work seamlessly with native pip without the heavyweight overhead of larger, more opinionated tools like Poetry or PDM. 
Pip tool come with compile and sync

#### Install pip tools 
```bash
    python -m pip install pip-tools
```

#### Explore pip tools
```bash
    python -m piptools
```

#### Pip Compile
The idea behind pip-compile is that we are going to generate a requirements.txt file from a requirements.in file. The requirements.in file will be the entry point for the developer and the requirements.txt file will then become a very exact representation of all the packages that your project needs.

- we create **requirements.in** with only need directly in projects
- then we run this **pip-compile requirements.in -o requirements.txt**
- i can change version in requirements and rerun the command
- and we generate file that look like this  : 
```txt
#
# This file is autogenerated by pip-compile with Python 3.11
# by the following command:
#
#    pip-compile --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
#
joblib==1.4.0
    # via scikit-learn
numpy==1.26.4
    # via
    #   scikit-learn
    #   scipy
scikit-learn==1.4.2
    # via -r requirements.in
scipy==1.13.0
    # via scikit-learn
threadpoolctl==3.4.0
    # via scikit-learn

```

#### Constraints
- The pipcompile command will respect constraints on project versions. 
- requirements.in : 
```
scikit-learn<1.3.0
django<2.2
```
- then run compile comand

- Then we can add **constraint**
- we can  have constraints across multiple input files. 
- This is incredibly useful when we want to seperate our deployment packages from your testing packages.
- we should then consider a **requirements-dev.in** :
```
    -c requirements.txt
    pytest==8.0.1
    django-debug-toolbar<2.2
```
- then we run :
```bash
    pip-compile requirements-dev.in -o requirements-dev.txt
```


#### HASHING
- to consider for security reason
```bash
    pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
```


#### Sync
- By using the sync command you'll not only install packages that aren't installed yet. You will also remove packages that should not be around! This is hugely beneficial when you're often installing packages locally to try them out. You can still keep using python -m pip install for every one-off, but then use the sync command to clean everything up.
- To give one example, imagine installing a new version of scipy which allows you to try out a new feature. It might be that this new version of scipy also demands that you upgrade numpy. This could, by accident, cause issues with scikit-learn. Even when you set the scipy package back to it's original version, this may not clean up the numpy dependency issues. All of this is very tedious to do manually, but the sync command will just handle all of this for your right from the get-go. It's very neat for local development!